node_modules/
dist/
dist-perf/
.shots/
# Measurements, but NOT .perf/baseline.json — see the negation below. A baseline
# is a claim about how fast this game is, and a claim like that belongs in the
# history next to the code it describes; everything else in here is one run's
# working out.
.perf/*
!.perf/baseline.json
.vite.log
*.local

# The TURN API token lives here. It is long-lived and can mint unlimited relay
# credentials on your account, so it must never be committed — see .env.example,
# which is the file that IS committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The ambience beds.
#
# This said "licensed from somewhere else — either way not ours to commit", and
# for the three recordings now in here that is NOT TRUE. Two are CC BY 4.0 and
# one is CC0 1.0; redistribution is exactly what those licences grant, and the
# attribution CC BY asks for is in CREDITS.md, which the build now emits into
# dist/. There is no licence reason to keep them out.
#
# The remaining reason is calibration, and it is the real one: `AmbienceBed.load`
# returns null when this directory is absent, so a stock checkout measures
# exactly the forest every script in scripts/ was tuned against. Committing the
# beds would move the baseline under all of them at once.
#
# ==> CONSEQUENCE, AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS: a fresh clone builds with NO BED AT
#     ALL, and nothing errors — `load` returning null is a supported state. A
#     deployed build would ship the forest silently missing its far chorus. If
#     Reality Room is ever deployed from a clean clone rather than from this
#     working copy, either this line comes out or the beds have to arrive some
#     other way. Left ignored deliberately; see CREDITS.md.
public/audio/beds/
